<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/6th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Goguryeo–Sui War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/598">598</a>–<a href="/years/614">614</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Goguryeo–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a> War was a series of failed invasions of the Korean kingdom of Goguryeo by the Sui dynasty in the early seventh century. The enormous losses and burdens fuelled rebellion and brought down the Sui, opening the way for the founding of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang Dynasty</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui dynasty</a> reunited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in 589 by defeating the Chen dynasty and then claimed to be overlord of its neighbours. King Pyeongwon of Goguryeo and his successor, Yeongyang, insisted on an equal relationship, and Goguryeo kept raiding the Sui frontier. In 596, after Sui envoys saw Goguryeo diplomats in the yurt of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-turkic-khaganate">Eastern Turkic Khaganate</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wen-of-sui">Emperor Wen of Sui</a> demanded that Goguryeo give up any alliance with the Turks, stop its raids and accept Sui as overlord. In 597 Yeongyang answered with a pre-emptive attack, together with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohe">Malgal</a>, on the border in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebei">Hebei</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In 598 Emperor Wen sent his youngest son, Yang Liang, and Admiral Zhou Luohou with 300,000 men by land and sea. Heavy rain, disease and constant attacks wore down the army, and the fleet lost many ships to rough seas and to Goguryeo raids whenever it anchored.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-yang-of-sui">Emperor Yang</a>, who had built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-canal">Grand Canal</a> after taking the throne in 604, assembled a far larger army; the Book of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a> counts 1,133,800 troops, though Tang historians may have exaggerated its size and losses. In 612 it crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-river">Liao River</a> on extended bridges, but Yodong and the other fortresses held, partly because the emperor required his generals to refer every decision, even a surrender, to him. A naval force under Lai Huni was ambushed by 500 Goguryeo troops while looting an outer castle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyongyang">Pyongyang</a>, and its 40,000 men were reduced to a few thousand.</p><p>The land army under Yu Zhongwen and Yuwen Shu ran short of food. The Goguryeo commander Ŭlchi Mundŏk drew it south with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feigned-retreat">feigned retreats</a> to within about 20 km of the capital, then offered terms. As it withdrew he attacked it at the Salsu, thought to be the Chongchon River, and of the 305,000 men who had crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao</a> only 2,700 returned. A campaign in 613 was broken off when Yang Xuangan rebelled, and in 614 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-yang-of-sui">Emperor Yang</a> accepted Yeongyang's offer of peace and the return of the fugitive Husi Zheng.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Yeongyang never came to swear allegiance, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-yang-of-sui">Emperor Yang</a> was dissuaded only with difficulty from another invasion. The losses in men and resources cost the dynasty the trust of its people, and peasants, soldiers, aristocrats and officers rose against it. Yang moved his capital south to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangzhou">Yangzhou</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuwen-huaji">Yuwen Huaji</a>, a son of Yuwen Shu, led a coup and killed him, and the Duke of Tang proclaimed a new dynasty.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Goguryeo–Sui War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Sui had been brought down by Emperor Yang's disastrous campaigns against the Korean kingdom of Goguryeo (the <b>Goguryeo–Sui War</b>) and the massive rebellions they provoked.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tang-dynasty">Tang Dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Their main purpose was to tap the resources of Jiangnan in the southeast to enrich the capital at Luoyang and to supply the Sui armies in the <b>Goguryeo–Sui War</b>.</blockquote><a href="/work/grand-canal-of-china">Grand Canal of China · How It Was Made</a></li></ul></section>
